course: dictionary
lesson: 4477
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words look the same but do different jobs. Harry with a capital H is a name, like Harry in «They went back together; Harry said sorry to the shopkeeper.» Harry with a small h is a verb, a doing word, and it means to keep attacking or annoying someone again and again.
explain: The capital letter is your first clue. Readers see a capital in the middle of a sentence and know it is a name, a person's name. Then check the words around it: if a doer is doing something, like «The dogs harried the sheep», the small-h harry is a verb.
explain: Harried can also be an adjective, a describing word. «A harried mum» is a busy mum who is stressed and rushed because she has too much to do. Ask yourself what the word is doing in the sentence: naming someone, acting, or describing?
fact: Harry with a capital H is a boy's or man's name, short for Henry or Harold.
fact: «Harry Potter» and «Prince Harry» are names, so they start with capital letters.
fact: harry with a small h is a verb: to keep attacking or annoying someone again and again.
fact: «The dogs harried the sheep» means the dogs kept chasing and bothering the sheep.
fact: harried describes someone who is stressed and rushed because of too much to do.
exercise: In «Harry also took a pound from Mum's purse for a lolly.», what kind of word is Harry? | A verb meaning to keep annoying | An adjective meaning stressed | *A name for a boy | A place
exercise: In «The dogs harried the sheep», what does harried mean? | Counted carefully | Gently stroked | Fed and rested | *Kept chasing and annoying again and again
exercise: What does «A harried mum» tell you about the mum? | She is calm and has nothing to do | *She is busy and stressed | She is fast asleep | She is a famous singer
test: Which sentence uses harry as a verb? | «Harry Potter is a book.» | «Harry said sorry to the shopkeeper.» | «Harry is my best friend.» | *«The wolves harried the cattle all night.»
test: A harried teacher on the last day of term is someone who is... | happy and relaxed | famous | bored and sleepy | *stressed and rushed
exam: Which word fills the gap in «The farmer's dog ___ the ducks all afternoon.»? | harvested | hairy | Harry | *harried
